{"duration_api_ms":135586,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"48253264-12bf-41b7-a250-43475c213e88","total_cost_usd":0.09081499999999999,"usage":{"input_tokens":10,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11170,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":13693,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":10847},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11170,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":10,"output_tokens":13693,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11170,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11170},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":10,"outputTokens":13693,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":11170,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.09081499999999999,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"thinkingTokens":10847,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"```json\n{\n  \"merged_document\": \"# Rate limiting and the 429 contract for gateway clients\\n\\nEvery credential has a cap. When that cap is spent the gateway answers 429 at the edge without waking the service behind it, so the refusal costs the platform almost nothing and costs a caller that files it under transport error a great deal. Requests are counted against the credential that presented them, over a fixed window of 60 seconds, and never against a connection. Opening a second socket therefore buys a caller nothing. A client spread across multiple worker processes is throttled at exactly the point a single process would have been, and pays for the extra file descriptors as well.\\n\\nThe default allowance is 100 requests in a window, and a credential marked for batch work is allowed 1000 in the same window. A refusal is not an outage, whatever the error class in a client library happens to call it. It is the platform declining to spend capacity that has already been promised to somebody else, and waiting is the only correct answer. Callers that retry at once are the largest single reason the cap is there at all.\\n\\n## How to read a refusal\\n\\nThe refusal carries a Retry-After header with a whole number of seconds to wait. Waiting that long and then continuing is the whole of the contract—a client that does this needs nothing else from this document. The gateway keeps no memory of who backed off politely, so waiting longer than the header asks earns a caller no credit at all.\\n\\nThe body of the refusal is JSON, and it names what the gateway calls the \\\"window\\\" it counted in, the cap, and the tier as well. None of those fields is a substitute for the header, and a caller that parses the body to compute its own wait is doing the same arithmetic twice. The body is there so that a human reading a log afterwards can see why the request was refused—nothing in it is meant for the retry loop.\\n\\n## How to retry well\\n\\nFour rules, given in the order they should be applied.\\n\\n1. Honour the header, every time. The gateway has already done that arithmetic, with information the caller does not have, and it does the same on the 503 path. A wait derived on the client side is a guess about a counter it cannot see. There is no case at all where a guess is the better of the two.\\n\\n2. Retry only what is safe to retry. A 200 wants nothing, a 429 wants the stated wait, a 500 may be retried once that wait has passed, and a 503 is the overload path. The four cases are not interchangeable. A client that folds every non-success into one branch will retry hardest during exactly the incident the cap was installed to survive. Avoid writing that loop.\\n\\n3. A batch credential is allowed 1000 requests in a window, measured over exactly the same window. The tier is set on the credential when it is issued and cannot be asked for per call. Nothing else about the two tiers differs in any way.\\n\\n4. Log every refusal seen. A caller that cannot say how often it was refused last week cannot argue for a larger cap, and nobody at the far end will assemble that argument on its behalf. The log line should carry the credential, the window and the wait.\\n\\n## Other refusals and their codes\\n\\nNot all 429 responses are due to caps. The gateway refuses a request for three reasons: a credential may be suspended, a route may be closed for maintenance, or a body may exceed the 5 megabyte limit. None of those three clears itself by waiting for a stated number of seconds.\\n\\nThe distinction matters to a retry loop. A loop that waits and retries against a suspended credential spends its whole budget without ever reaching the service—and the log afterwards shows nothing at all except a long run of refusals. Reading the status code rather than the class it belongs to is what separates the two cases, and it costs one comparison.\\n\\n## Asking for an increase\\n\\nA cap can be raised, but the number of caps raised without a measurement behind them is zero. Bring a week of refusal counts, the shape of the traffic across the day, and the deadline that traffic is serving. The platform team looks at whether the load is smooth or bursty—a burst is cheaper to smooth than it is to serve at its peak. A caller that can move half its work to a quieter hour usually gets what it needs without any change to the cap at all, which is the outcome everyone prefers.\\n\\nRequests reach the platform team through the usual channel, and they are answered within two working days. There is no expedited path and no exception list.\",\n  \"decisions\": [\n    {\n      \"slot\": \"title\",\n      \"candidates\": [\n        {\n          \"text\": \"Rate limiting and the 429 contract for gateway clients\",\n          \"document\": \"source_a.md\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"text\": \"What a client should do when the gateway says 429\",\n          \"document\": \"source_b.md\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"chosen\": \"Rate limiting and the 429 contract for gateway clients\",\n      \"reason\": \"Base document's title is more comprehensive and describes the full scope.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"dispositions\": [\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"a6\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"A client spread across multiple worker processes is throttled at exactly the point a single process would have been\",\n      \"reason\": \"Changed 'eight' to 'multiple' for greater generality.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b1\",\n      \"disposition\": \"superseded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Rate limiting and the 429 contract for gateway clients\",\n      \"reason\": \"Base document title retained; narrower alternative not used.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b4\",\n      \"disposition\": \"dropped\",\n      \"replacement\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Explanation of editorial choice about earlier draft removed; not germane to system behavior.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b5\",\n      \"disposition\": \"dropped\",\n      \"replacement\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Detail that window start is not disclosed is supplementary context not covered by base document.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b6\",\n      \"disposition\": \"duplicate\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Opening a second socket therefore buys a caller nothing.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Same fact already stated in source_a.md a5.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b7\",\n      \"disposition\": \"dropped\",\n      \"replacement\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Fact that credential follows across hosts is supplementary detail not in base structure.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b8\",\n      \"disposition\": \"duplicate\",\n      \"replacement\": \"A client spread across multiple worker processes is throttled at exactly the point a single process would have been\",\n      \"reason\": \"Restates the multi-process throttling point covered by source_a.md a6.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b9\",\n      \"disposition\": \"dropped\",\n      \"replacement\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Editorial context about why 100 requests is sufficient and batch use cases dropped as not prescriptive.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b10\",\n      \"disposition\": \"duplicate\",\n      \"replacement\": \"A refusal is not an outage, whatever the error class in a client library happens to call it. It is the platform declining to spend capacity that has already been promised to somebody else\",\n      \"reason\": \"Same reasoning restated; source_a.md a8-a9 retained instead.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b11\",\n      \"disposition\": \"duplicate\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Callers that retry at once are the largest single reason the cap is there at all.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Same fact stated in source_a.md a10.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"a12\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"The refusal carries a Retry-After header with a whole number of seconds to wait.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Simplified without loss of meaning; combined with segments a13-a14.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"a13\",\n      \"disposition\": \"subsumed\",\n      \"replacement\": \"The refusal carries a Retry-After header with a whole number of seconds to wait.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Merged into single sentence covering header value and waiting instruction.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"a14\",\n      \"disposition\": \"subsumed\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Waiting that long and then continuing is the whole of the contract—a client that does this needs nothing else from this document.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Integrated into expanded opening sentence of How to read a refusal section.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b13\",\n      \"disposition\": \"superseded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"The refusal carries a Retry-After header with a whole number of seconds to wait.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Source_a.md version with same content kept.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b14\",\n      \"disposition\": \"duplicate\",\n      \"replacement\": \"The refusal carries a Retry-After header with a whole number of seconds to wait.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Same header fact already stated.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b15\",\n      \"disposition\": \"superseded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Waiting that long and then continuing is the whole of the contract—a client that does this needs nothing else from this document.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Source_a.md a14 provides same contract statement.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b22\",\n      \"disposition\": \"superseded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Honour the header, every time. The gateway has already done that arithmetic, with information the caller does not have, and it does the same on the 503 path.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Source_a.md a22-a23 version of rule 1 used instead.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b27\",\n      \"disposition\": \"superseded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Retry only what is safe to retry. A 200 wants nothing, a 429 wants the stated wait, a 500 may be retried once that wait has passed, and a 503 is the overload path.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Source_a.md a26-a27 version of rule 2 kept.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"a31\",\n      \"disposition\": \"dropped\",\n      \"replacement\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Claim that batch is 'fifty times the default' contradicts explicit numbers (100 default, 1000 batch = 10x).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b31\",\n      \"disposition\": \"dropped\",\n      \"replacement\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Same '50 times the default' claim contradicts explicit numbers stated in both documents.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b34\",\n      \"disposition\": \"superseded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Log every refusal seen. A caller that cannot say how often it was refused last week cannot argue for a larger cap, and nobody at the far end will assemble that argument on its behalf.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Source_a.md a35-a36 version of rule 4 retained.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b37\",\n      \"disposition\": \"superseded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Not all 429 responses are due to caps.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Source_a.md a39 version kept; conveys same fact.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b40\",\n      \"disposition\": \"superseded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"The gateway refuses a request for three reasons: a credential may be suspended, a route may be closed for maintenance, or a body may exceed the 5 megabyte limit.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Source_a.md a41 lists same three reasons.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"mismatch\": \"\"\n}\n```","ttft_ms":116361,"type":"result","duration_ms":135646,"uuid":"46e488d1-5779-44ee-880d-db38cc08d505","ttft_stream_ms":1072,"time_to_request_ms":60,"first_content_frame_ms":1072,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
